WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:04.420
Iran is unique in the world today

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.860
it has tens of thousands of victims

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.740
of chemical weapons weapons use

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.980
during the iran-iraq war in the 1980s

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.820
sulfur mustard, nerve agents for the first time

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.060
were used on the battlefield in that war

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:19.200
it's been more than thirty years since these victims

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.000
were exposed to the chemicals and now

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.540
scientists are unraveling the long-term
effects

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.900
and also looking at the

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.740
basic mechanisms of chemical weapons

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.260
and how they affect the body initially the acute

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.260
effects and then in the long term

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.080
as these consequences happen many years later

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.360
I am Richard stone international
news editor for science

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.240
I went to Iran to report on survivors of

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.280
chemical weapons attacks in a project sponsored

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.480
by the Pulitzer Center for Crisis Reporting

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.040
The first large-scale use of chemical weapons

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.400
was during World War one

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.400
That was mostly mustard and chlorine

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.260
And it was many years after that

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.520
that chemical weapons were used on a large scale in

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.340
the battlefield and that took place

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.080
during the Iran-Iraq war

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.160
Sulfur mustard is very corrosive chemical

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.680
it's not like the nerve agents

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.160
it's been around for more than a century

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.480
it was used in WWI but its mechanism of action is still

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.880
rather opaque it blisters so

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.400
if you get on the skin it forms these huge blisters

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.880
called bullae, if you breathe it in

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.880
it attacks the lung cells and if you get

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.520
into your eyes it can also cause quite big damage there

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.080
as an oily substance

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.040
it is absorbed quite easily

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.920
so if you survive sulfur mustard it

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.560
takes a while for you to heal

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.280
the blisters take months to heal

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.600
Many years later people are discovering that

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.960
the lung damage that was caused by

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.440
the acute exposure

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.680
it sets a molecular mechanism in motion

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.200
like a little time bomb that explodes
later

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.640
into very life-threatening symptoms

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.680
the lung tissue breaking down,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.320
you become more prone to lung infections

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.080
and a lot of people succumb

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.280
months or years later after the exposure

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.240
to these effects and the mechanism

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.440
molecular mechanism of these effects is

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.400
still being worked out

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:24.200
so Iran has a huge population of mustard victims who

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.400
are still suffering symptoms

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.040
tens of thousands of people

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.800
it's a unique cohort

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.200
to try to develop better strategies

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.720
to treat mustard and also if there's some way to

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.000
develop chemicals, compounds

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.720
that can be applied during the initial attack

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.040
right now that antidote to sulfur mustard does not exist

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.280
all nerve agents attack an enzyme

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.800
called acetylcholinesterase

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.240
it's vital to the body

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.640
it breaks down the neurotransmitter acetylcholine

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.680
so it is essential for maintaining levels of

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.920
the neurotransmitter in the synapses

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.920
one of the most insidious effects of nerve agents

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.320
is that after they bind to acetylcholinesterase

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.400
this bond over time hardens,

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.320
becomes permanent essentially

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.000
and this is called aging

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.120
so as acetylcholine builds up in the synapses

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:36.240
it over stimulates the nerves cells

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.440
and that has a variety of effects so it

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.760
can lead to seizures, convulsions

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.640
when the nerve agent enters the brain and

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.000
starts interacting with the enzyme there

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.840
it sends victims into a coma and unless

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.480
they're treated really quickly

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.480
they will usually stop breathing

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.240
so soldiers who are exposed

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.800
to nerve agents carry three countermeasures

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.880
that they can self-inject or inject into a buddy

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.880
one is called atropine,

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.040
it binds to the same enzyme that the nerve agents

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.640
bind to, therefore preventing the nerve agent

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.640
from exerting its effects

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.600
another is an oxime that

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.360
if a nerve agent is attached to the enzyme

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.520
it pries it off, so it's like a little molecular crowbar

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.640
and the third is an anticonvulsant

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:33.040
one of the major symptoms of nerve agent exposure is

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:38.240
seizures and this anticonvulsant takes care of that.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.080
for a long time people thought that if you're

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.080
exposed to a nerve agent,

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.000
you either lived or died

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.160
if you survived the nerve agent attack

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.440
you went on to live a normal, average, healthy life,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.880
it didn't have long-term effects people presumed

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.080
that began to change especially,

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.560
after the Tokyo terror attacks

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.120
it was clearly documented that people

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.040
exposed to sarin did suffer long-term consequences.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.280
That's a new revelation and

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.240
Iranian scientists see an opportunity

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.840
now to try to track down

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.840
victims of the nerve agent attacks

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.800
and monitor their current health and

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.680
see if they can contribute to the developing picture

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.960
of the long-term effects of nerve agents

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.520
so this is a unique resource for scientists

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.400
it's a very tragic story for the victims

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.680
but to be able to study using modern tools,

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:32.760
the effects of chemical weapons on the human body

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.320
this is the only place right now

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.080
where scientists can do that

